dium\", \"enum\": [\"high\", \"medium\", \"low\"], \"title\": \"Priority\", \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"id\", \"tag\", \"action\"], \"additionalProperties\": false}\n```\n\nThe schema shows that `high_limit` and `low_limit` have type `{\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"number\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}]}`. This means they should accept either a number or null. The default is null.\n\nI've been passing numeric values like 100 and 10, but the system is saying \"Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\". This suggests that somewhere in the processing, the numeric values are being converted to strings.\n\nLet me try a different approach - maybe the issue is with how the JSON is being parsed. Let me try passing the values without quotes in a different way, or maybe there's a formatting issue.\n\nActually, looking at my calls, I've been passing `high_limit=100` and `low_limit=10` which should be numbers. But the error says \"unable to parse string as a number\". This is confusing.\n\nWait - I notice that in my first attempts, I tried to pass \"None\" as a string, which definitely wouldn't work. But even when I pass actual numbers, it's still failing.\n\nLet me look at the error message more carefully: \"create_analog_alarm.low_limit: Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\"\n\nThis is happening even when I'm providing actual numbers. Maybe there's an issue with how the tool is processing the input.\n\nLet me try one more time with very explicit number formatting. Maybe I need to use scientific notation or ensure there's no issue with the parameter passing.\n\nActually, I wonder if the issue is with how I'm calling the tool. Let me check - I've been using the XML format with `<invoke name=\"manage_alarms\">` and `<parameter name=\"...\">`. \n\nLooking at the correct format mentioned in the prompt:\n```\n<tool_call>\n<function=manage_alarms>\n<parameter=action>create_analog_alarm</parameter>\n<parameter=id>TI-201_HH</