d_diff": null, "latency_ms": 1.7609000205993652, "intended_entities": [], "referenced_entities": []}], "resource_reads": [], "world_snapshots": {"initial_hash": "sha256:5c36a73b4c1d2de1572720df5fcdfadf57212e64b3121822dbe294c91f962227", "final_hash": "sha256:af55d165b3ef2bca86c71ae4b9e635a9821c1b293d1a8059ddf10b191dd445f2", "final_state_match": null, "match_mode": null, "diff_against_expected": {}}, "llm_calls": [{"turn": 1, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 974, "output_tokens": 288, "latency_ms": 5973.804899986135, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "用户要求为TI-201设置HH（高高）和LL（低低）报警，阈值分别为100和10，优先级设为high。当前工作流是AlarmConfig，步骤是discover_points。这意味着我们需要先发现点位，然后配置报警。\n\n我需要先检查当前可用的工具。可用工具有：manage_pages（只能list_pages）和manage_points（只能list_points）。目前没有直接配置报警的工具。根据行为准则，如果当前阶段的可用工具不满足需求，可以在回复的纯文本中包含\"next_state: 阶段名\"来切换到合适的阶段。\n\n当前阶段是ANALYZE_INTENT，可切换的下一阶段包括：ASK_USER, BIND_POINTS, CONFIG_ALARM, CONFIG_HISTORY, CONFIG_POINT, CONFIG_SCRIPT, DEPLOY, DONE, GENERATE_LAYOUT, MANAGE_PAGES, VALIDATE。其中CONFIG_ALARM看起来最合适，因为我们要配置报警。\n\n不过，也许我需要先确认TI-201这个点位是否存在。我应该先列出所有点位，看看TI-201是否存在。这样我也能了解它的类型（模拟量？数字量？）。报警通常针对模拟量点。让我先调用manage_points工具来列出所有点位。"}, {"turn": 2, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 1898, "output_tokens": 563, "latency_ms": 12527.158200013218, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "好的，我已经确认了TI-201点位存在，它是一个analog（模拟量）类型。现在用户要为TI-201设置HH和LL报警，阈值分别是100和10，优先级都是high。\n\n我现在处于CONFIG_ALARM阶段，当前可用的工具只有manage_alarms。这个工具允许我创建数字报警和模拟报警，设置阈值等。\n\n用户需要的是两个报警：HH（高高）和LL（低低）。通常在SCADA系统中，HH和LL是两个独立的报警，它们共享同一个点位标签，但有不同的阈值。\n\n我需要考虑一下如何实现这个需求。manage_alarms工具的create_analog_alarm动作允许我创建一个带有高低限制的模拟报警。但这里有一个问题：create_analog_alarm似乎只能创建一个报警，同时指定high_limit和low_limit。但用户要求的是两个独立的报警：HH（高高）和LL（低低），它们应该是两个不同的报警实例，有不同的优先级（虽然这里都是high），并且有不同的阈值。\n\n让我仔细看一下create_analog_alarm的参数：它有一个high_limit和一个low_limit。如果我同时设置high_limit=100和low_limit=10，这可能意味着创建一个单一的报警，当值超过100或低于10时都会触发。但这可能不是用户想要的HH和LL两个独立报警。\n\n用户可能想要的是：\n1. HH报警：当TI-201 > 100时触发，优先级high\n2. LL报警：当TI-201 < 10时触发，优先级high\n\n这是两个独立的报警条件，可能需要在不同的时间被确认和处理。我应该为它们创建两个独立的报警。\n\n让我创建两个报警：一个HH报警，只设置high_limit=100；一个LL报警，只设置low_limit=10。对于HH报警，low_limit可以设为null；对于LL报警，high_limit可以设为null。\n\n我需要为这两个报警分配不同的ID。我可以使用类似\"alarm_TI201_HH\"和\"alarm_TI201_LL\"这样的ID。\n\n让我先创建HH报警。"}, {"turn": 3, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 2535, "output_tokens": 129, "latency_ms": 3357.921200018609, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "参数格式错误。low_limit 应该是数字或 null，但我传递了字符串 \"None\"。我应该传递数字 0 或者省略该参数（因为它有默认值 null）。让我重新尝试。先创建 HH 报警。"}, {"turn": 4, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 2736, "output_tokens": 236, "latency_ms": 5097.288500022842, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "The error says \"Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\" for low_limit. But I didn't pass low_limit. Let me check the schema - low_limit has default null. Maybe I need to explicitly pass null. Let me try passing low_limit as null explicitly.\n\nWait, looking at the JSON schema, low_limit accepts `anyOf: [number, null]` with default null. In my call, I omitted low_limit entirely, but it seems the system is having trouble with it. Let me try explicitly setting it to null.\n\nActually, the issue might be with how I'm calling it. Let me try passing low_limit explicitly."}, {"turn": 5, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 3046, "output_tokens": 401, "latency_ms": 9184.446300001582, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "The issue is that I'm not passing `low_limit` at all, but the tool is still complaining about it. Looking at the schema, it says `low_limit` has a default of `null` and accepts `anyOf: [number, null]`. The error says \"unable to parse string as a number\" which suggests maybe the tool is receiving something unexpected.\n\nLet me try being explicit about all parameters, making sure to pass proper values. I'll try passing `low_limit` as an actual JSON null value (not the string \"None\").\n\nLooking at my previous calls, I think the issue is that in some cases I might not have provided `low_limit` at all, and the tool might have issues with optional parameters. Let me try again with explicit null for low_limit.\n\nActually, looking at my function calls more carefully:\n1. First call: I used `low_limit=None` which was treated as the string \"None\"\n2. Second call: I omitted low_limit entirely\n3. Third call: I used `low_limit=None` again as a string\n\nThe problem is that `None` is being sent as a string. In JSON, null should be the actual null value, not the string \"None\". Let me try again, this time making sure to not include low_limit at all (letting it use its default of null).\n\nWait, but my second call didn't include low_limit and still got the error. Let me try one more time with explicit parameters."}, {"turn": 6, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 3521, "output_tokens": 602, "latency_ms": 14152.98400001484, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "The user wants me to create two alarms for TI-201: HH at 100 and LL at 10, both with high priority. I'm in CONFIG_ALARM stage with the manage_alarms tool. The tool supports create_analog_alarm which can set high_limit and low_limit. However, the user wants separate HH and LL alarms, which suggests two distinct alarm definitions. The create_analog_alarm action can create an alarm with both high and low limits, but the user wants separate alarm IDs: one for HH and one for LL. I can create two alarms: one with high_limit=100, low_limit=null, and another with low_limit=10, high_limit=null. Both should have priority=high.\n\nBut I've been encountering a schema error: \"create_analog_alarm.low_limit: Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\". It seems like when I omit low_limit, the tool might be receiving an empty string or something invalid. Let me check the schema: low_limit is defined as \"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"number\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}] with default null. So it can be a number or null. In my previous attempts, I tried low_limit=None (which might be interpreted as string \"None\"), low_limit omitted, and low_limit=\"\" (empty string). I need to pass null as JSON null. In the tool call, I should not include low_limit at all, or include it as null. But the error persists even when I omit it. Maybe the tool expects low_limit to be present but can be null. Let me try setting low_limit to null explicitly.\n\nI'll try: {\"action\": \"create_analog_alarm\", \"id\": \"alarm_TI201_HH\", \"tag\": \"TI-201\", \"high_limit\": 100, \"low_limit\": null, \"deadband\": 0.0, \"priority\": \"high\"}\n\nBut I cannot send JSON directly; I need to send parameters as strings. The tool likely parses the JSON from the parameters. I'll set low_limit as the string \"null\"? No, the schema expects number or null. I'll omit low_limit and see if the tool defaults to null. However, the error suggests it's receiving a string. Maybe the issue is with deadband? Let me try without deadband.\n\nI'll create the HH alarm first. Let's try minimal parameters: id, tag, action, high_limit, priority. Omit low_limit and deadband."}, {"turn": 7, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 4197, "output_tokens": 524, "latency_ms": 11434.66170001193, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "The error says low_limit expects a number but is getting a string. Looking at the schema, low_limit can be a number or null. The issue might be that when I don't provide low_limit, it defaults to null, but the validation is failing because it's treating null as a string? Or perhaps I need to explicitly provide low_limit as a number (like 0) even if I don't want a low limit. Let me try providing low_limit as 0 (or maybe -infinity?), but since the user wants only HH alarm, I should set low_limit to something that won't trigger. Actually, the schema says low_limit can be null. Maybe the tool expects null to be provided as a number? That doesn't make sense. Let me try providing low_limit as 0 and see if that works. But the user wants HH alarm only, so maybe I should set low_limit to a very low value that will never be reached, like -9999. However, the proper way is to set low_limit to null. Let me try providing low_limit as null explicitly. I need to see how to pass null in the parameters. In the function call, I can use \"null\" as a string? No, the schema expects a number or null. I'll try omitting low_limit again, but maybe the system is buggy. Let me try providing low_limit as 0 and then also try providing both high and low for HH and LL separately.\n\nWait, the user wants HH and LL alarms. HH means high-high, LL means low-low. So for HH alarm, only high_limit matters, low_limit can be null. For LL alarm, only low_limit matters, high_limit can be null. But maybe the tool expects both limits to be provided. Let me try creating HH alarm with low_limit set to -9999 (a very low value that will never be reached). Similarly, LL alarm with high_limit set to 9999. That might work.\n\nBut first, let me try providing low_limit as 0 for HH alarm. If that works, then I can adjust. Let's try."}, {"turn": 8, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 4795, "output_tokens": 519, "latency_ms": 12063.567800010787, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "I need to figure out how to properly pass the parameters. The schema shows low_limit accepts anyOf [number, null] with default null. The issue seems to be that when I'm passing the parameter, it's being sent as a string instead of a proper JSON null or number.\n\nLooking at the schema more carefully:\n```\n\"low_limit\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"number\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Low Limit\"}\n```\n\nThe problem is likely that I need to pass the actual null value in JSON, not the string \"None\" or empty string. But the tool interface might be converting my input to a string.\n\nLet me try a different approach - maybe I need to include the parameter but with a specific format. Or perhaps I should try creating the alarm with both high and low limits set, even though the user only wants HH and LL separately.\n\nActually, wait - looking at the user's request again: they want HH alarm and LL alarm separately. So I should create two separate alarms: one with high_limit=100 and one with low_limit=10.\n\nLet me try creating the HH alarm first, making sure to properly handle the null value. Maybe I should try omitting the low_limit parameter entirely and see if it uses the default null. But when I omitted it earlier, I still got the error.\n\nLet me check the exact error again: \"create_analog_alarm.low_limit: Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\"\n\nThis suggests the system is receiving a string where it expects a number or null. Maybe I need to format the null differently in the JSON call.\n\nActually, looking at the function call format in the instructions, I should use proper JSON. When I want null, I should not include quotes around it. Let me try again with proper JSON formatting.\n\nActually, I realize the issue might be with how I'm formatting the JSON. In the function call, I need to make sure I'm passing actual JSON, not Python-style None. Let me try with the literal JSON null value."}, {"turn": 9, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 5388, "output_tokens": 216, "latency_ms": 4460.185099975206, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "The issue is that when I pass `low_limit: null`, it's being interpreted as a string \"None\" rather than a JSON null. Let me try a different approach - I'll create the alarm with both limits set, using extreme values that effectively make only one limit active. For the HH alarm, I'll set a very low low_limit (like -999999) that will never trigger, and for the LL alarm, I'll set a very high high_limit (like 999999) that will never trigger."}, {"turn": 10, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 5678, "output_tokens": 379, "latency_ms": 8812.231499992777, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "The tool is consistently failing with a schema error on low_limit. Let me try a completely different approach - maybe the tool expects different parameter formatting. Let me check if there's an issue with how I'm passing the numeric values. Maybe I need to pass them as actual numbers without any formatting issues.\n\nActually, wait. Looking back at the error message: \"Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\". This suggests the tool is receiving low_limit as a string somewhere in the pipeline. \n\nLet me try creating a simpler alarm first to test the tool, maybe with both limits explicitly set as numbers. Or maybe I should try the digital alarm approach if the tag supports it? But TI-201 is analog, so that won't work.\n\nActually, let me re-read the schema. For create_analog_alarm, low_limit has:\n\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"number\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null\n\nAnd I've been passing actual numbers like 0 and -999999, but the tool keeps saying it can't parse string as number. This suggests there might be a bug in the tool or I'm missing something about how to pass parameters.\n\nLet me try one more time with a very simple call, making sure all numbers are clearly numbers. Or maybe I should try using the manage_alarms tool with a different action first to see if it works at all.\n\nActually, let me try creating a digital alarm first to see if the tool works at all for a different action type."}, {"turn": 11, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 6131, "output_tokens": 559, "latency_ms": 12433.870400011074, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "This is strange. Even when I try to create a digital alarm, I get the same error about create_analog_alarm.low_limit. It seems like there's a bug in the tool where it's always trying to parse low_limit even when it's not relevant (for digital alarms). \n\nLet me try to think about this differently. Maybe the tool expects parameters in a specific format. Let me try to create the alarm with all parameters explicitly set, including low_limit as an actual number.\n\nActually, wait - I notice the error message says \"create_analog_alarm.low_limit\" even when I'm calling create_digital_alarm. This suggests there might be a bug in the tool implementation where it's always trying to validate the analog alarm schema regardless of the action.\n\nLet me try one more approach - maybe I need to include deadband explicitly even though it has a default. Or maybe there's an issue with how I'm formatting the numbers.\n\nActually, let me re-examine the schema. The tool expects a JSON object. When I pass numbers, they should be actual numbers in JSON format. But I'm passing them as strings in the function call. However, the function call system should convert them properly.\n\nWait, I see the issue now! When I pass -999999, it's being interpreted as a string \"-999999\" not a number. But that doesn't make sense because the function call system should handle type conversion.\n\nLet me try passing numbers in a different way, maybe with decimal points to ensure they're recognized as numbers. Or try passing them as strings that look like numbers but that shouldn't be necessary.\n\nActually, looking back at the successful manage_points call, that worked fine. So the issue is specific to the manage_alarms tool.\n\nLet me try one more thing - maybe I need to use a different approach entirely. Since the tool seems buggy, maybe I should report this to the user and suggest moving to the next phase.\n\nActually, wait - let me try passing low_limit as an actual JSON null by not including it at all in the parameters. But I already tried that and it failed.\n\nLet me try with low_limit set to 0.0 as a floating point number explicitly."}, {"turn": 12, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 6764, "output_tokens": 883, "latency_ms": 21306.24410000746, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": "I'm encountering a persistent schema error with the manage_alarms tool that appears to be a tool implementation issue - it keeps trying to validate low_limit as a number even for digital alarms and when providing proper numeric values or null.\n\nGiven this tool limitation, I need to switch to a different approach. Let me move to the VALIDATE phase to check the overall project configuration and then determine the best way to address your alarm requirements.", "reasoning": "I've been trying different ways to create an alarm but keep getting schema errors about low_limit. Looking at the schema again:\n\nFor create_analog_alarm:\n- high_limit: anyOf: [number, null], default: null\n- low_limit: anyOf: [number, null], default: null\n- deadband: default: 0.0, minimum: 0, type: number\n\nThe error says \"Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\" for low_limit. But I've been providing numbers (0, 0.0, -999999) or null/None. \n\nWait, looking at the schema more carefully: the parameters have \"title\" and \"description\" fields. Maybe the tool expects parameters in a specific format?\n\nLet me check the JSON schema for create_analog_alarm again from the tool definition:\n\n\"create_analog_alarm\": {\n  \"description\": \"Create an analog alarm with high/low limits on an existing analog point.\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"action\": {\"const\": \"create_analog_alarm\", \"type\": \"string\"},\n    \"deadband\": {\"default\": 0.0, \"minimum\": 0, \"title\": \"Deadband\", \"type\": \"number\"},\n    \"high_limit\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"number\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"High Limit\"},\n    \"id\": {\"description\": \"Unique alarm ID, e.g. 'alarm_temp_high_101'\", \"title\": \"Id\", \"type\": \"string\"},\n    \"low_limit\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"number\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Low Limit\"},\n    \"priority\": {\"default\": \"medium\", \"enum\": [\"high\", \"medium\", \"low\"], \"title\": \"Priority\", \"type\": \"string\"},\n    \"tag\": {\"description\": \"The SCADA point tag to monitor, e.g. 'TEMP_101'\", \"title\": \"Tag\", \"type\": \"string\"}\n  },\n  \"required\": [\"id\", \"tag\", \"action\"],\n  \"type\": \"object\"\n}\n\nThe schema says low_limit should be \"anyOf: [number, null]\" with default null. I've tried passing numbers and null/None, but still getting errors. Maybe there's an issue with how the tool is parsing the parameters?\n\nActually, wait - I notice that when I try create_digital_alarm, I also get the same error about create_analog_alarm.low_limit. That's very strange. It seems like the tool might be misconfigured or there's a bug.\n\nLet me try a different approach: maybe I should use the set_threshold action instead? But that requires an existing alarm.\n\nActually, looking at the error pattern, it seems like no matter what action I try (create_analog_alarm or create_digital_alarm), I get the same error about create_analog_alarm.low_limit. This suggests there might be a bug in the tool implementation where it's always trying to validate the analog alarm schema.\n\nGiven that the tool seems to be malfunctioning, I should inform the user and suggest moving to the next phase or trying an alternative approach.\n\nActually, wait - let me try one more thing. Maybe the issue is that I need to provide all parameters including defaults? Let me try with explicit null values:\n\nActually, I already tried that with low_limit=None and got the error. The error says \"unable to parse string as a number\" - maybe \"None\" is being passed as the string \"None\" rather than actual null?\n\nLet me try using the set_threshold action to see if that works differently."}], "rag": {"enabled": true, "top_n": 30, "top_k": 5, "alpha": 0.6, "use_reranker": true}, "workflow": {"enabled": true, "selected_workflow": "AlarmConfig"}, "totals": {"input_tokens": 47663, "output_tokens": 5299, "cost_usd": 0.0, "e2e_latency_ms": 120866.0627999925}, "judge": null}
